---

Journal entries are the quietest kind of post: no table of contents unless you want one, no cover image, just words and the occasional photo. This one is about a slow weekend.

## What happened

I spent most of Saturday rebuilding this site's reading experience instead of going outside. It was raining anyway. By the afternoon I had a working focus mode, which hides everything except the text, and I used it right away to read an article I had saved for weeks.

![A placeholder photo from the weekend](../../assets/writing/example-wide.svg "A photo from the weekend. Journal entries can carry images too, and they open full size on click.")

Sunday was the opposite. I walked for two hours, took too few pictures, and came back with a list of ideas I promptly forgot.

> The best way to finish something is to stop working on it for a day.

That line has been on a sticky note above my desk for a year. It is more true than it is comfortable.

## What I learned

- Small rituals beat big plans
- Writing things down makes them *real* enough to act on
- Reading in a quiet layout is a different activity than reading in a busy one

## What I want to try next

1. Write a post every two weeks, even a short one
2. Add a photo to each entry
3. Spend one weekend completely offline

---

That is all for now. See you after the next rainy weekend.
